WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:07.200
Isaiah Berlin distinguishes&nbsp;
in his book “Two Concepts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.800
Of Liberty” between negative&nbsp;
liberty and positive liberty.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:18.900
Negative liberty describes the individual&nbsp;
freedom from something: from external constraint,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:24.480
and from outside interference. It’s the&nbsp;
absence of obstacles within a society.

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:33.900
Positive liberty means freedom to do something:&nbsp;
to act upon one’s will, to take control of one’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:40.140
life. It's the possession of the power to proceed&nbsp;
regardless of structural limitations in society.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:47.700
Sounds confusing? The problems of two&nbsp;
imaginary people illustrate this difference.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:55.140
This is Frank. He lives in Freeland&nbsp;
with the two things he loves most:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.140 --> 00:01:00.300
his kids and his weights. And then&nbsp;
there is Lilly. She lives in Liberty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:04.500
values justice above all, and&nbsp;
dreams of becoming a judge.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:07.380
But both face lots of obstacles.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:15.420
To Frank, there is nothing more important&nbsp;
than his health and his family. He gets up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:21.060
very early for his routines and then takes the&nbsp;
children to school. He pays strict attention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:26.460
to his diet and his kids' education and&nbsp;
looks down on people who neglect both.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:35.340
He doesn’t like the authorities. In Freeland, they&nbsp;
collect lots of taxes to fund the public health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:40.980
care system. Why pay the medical bills for those&nbsp;
that don’t care about their health, he wonders.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:44.280
Frank seeks freedom from these taxes.

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:52.440
Frank loves to lift weights in front of his&nbsp;
house once the kids are in bed. Yet recently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:58.800
Freeland prohibited working out in public&nbsp;
after 10 pm. For Frank, that’s none of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:04.380
government's business. He wants freedom from&nbsp;
laws that restrict his recreational habits.

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:06.480
And there is one more thing.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:14.100
Frank also doesn’t like public schools.&nbsp;
He thinks they are useless and would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:19.980
prefer to teach his kids at home. But&nbsp;
in Freeland homeschooling is illegal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:25.320
Frank thinks that is outrageous and&nbsp;
wants freedom from compulsory education.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:32.280
Eventually, he’s had enough and moves&nbsp;
to Liberty where the taxes are lower,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:38.400
the government smaller, and where he believes he’s&nbsp;
got greater freedom from external constraints.

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:42.480
Lilly is already there and feels trapped.

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:51.060
Lilly recently tripped and broke her leg. Now&nbsp;
she needs surgery but medical treatments are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:57.240
expensive in Liberty. To pay for it, Lilly has&nbsp;
to take out a loan. She thinks the system is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:02.580
unfair and feels she doesn’t have the freedom&nbsp;
to receive decent affordable health care.

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:04.740
And things get worse.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:13.680
After her surgery, Lilly needs to rehabilitate&nbsp;
and takes walks around the block. This is when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:19.260
she realizes that she hates the smoke from&nbsp;
all the cars on the street. The smog she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:25.620
inhales is clearly dangerous. Lilly finds&nbsp;
she has no freedom to recover appropriately.

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:33.420
This injustice reminds Lilly day in and&nbsp;
day out that she wants to become a judge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:38.100
But since she’s from a disadvantaged&nbsp;
family, she can’t afford the tuition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:43.620
fees of a university. And to take out&nbsp;
another loan scares her. Eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:50.460
she just feels frustrated and bitter. She&nbsp;
thinks she has no freedom to pursue her dreams.

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:57.900
Lilly isn’t happy in Liberty and moves&nbsp;
to Freeland with a stronger welfare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:04.500
state. A place where she has the power to act&nbsp;
regardless of structural limitations in society.

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:10.800
Supporters of negative liberty&nbsp;
tend to sympathize with classical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:16.020
libertarian thoughts. They think of&nbsp;
the state as a coercive force that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:21.600
limits individual freedom and prefer a small&nbsp;
government with few taxes and regulations.

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:31.500
Advocates of positive liberty tend to identify as&nbsp;
social liberals. They usually want a big welfare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:37.380
state that ensures a fair redistribution of&nbsp;
wealth and provides equal access to opportunities.

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:46.620
What about you? Which kind of freedom do you want&nbsp;
for yourself? And do you think Isaiah Berlin’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:53.040
theory, “the two concepts of liberty”, is right or&nbsp;
wrong? Share your thoughts in the comments below!

